A||||8||Mark||headliner replacement||||||
Z||000000||Mark||07-27-99||09:07 PM||||I'm going to replace my headliner soon (2nd attempt). In my first attempt (failure), I used contact cement, but probably didn't use enough on the headliner material.  This time, I'm using 3M Super Trim Adhesive (and new material of course). Any tips or suggestions?  My concern is the material must be stretched a considerable amount to conform to the "dished" areas above the driver's and passenger's heads.  Does this sound normal? 
Z||000001||batboy||07-27-99||09:43 PM||||You are a braver guy than me. I took my Fiero to the upholstery shop, shelled out a few bucks and let them do it. Looks great. Sometimes ya just gotta pay the price and let the pros do the job.
Z||000002||lowCG||07-28-99||12:53 AM||||My car has a "sky is falling" headliner,was going to use 3M #58 adhesive,from the hardware store, a lot more aggressive than contact cement.<br>If that doesn't work out,maybe I'll farm it out,good idea sometimes,just because.
Z||000003||DJRice||07-28-99||08:23 AM||||I have to agree with batboy about the upholstery shop.  I dont mind dismantling an engine or working on brakes or any mechanical stuff, but If I want my headliner done or windows tinted, I take it to someone who does it all the time.  
Z||000004||Carrolles||07-30-99||12:58 PM||||It's not a hard job.  I've done just about every GM car I've owned.<p>You need the adhesive made for Foam Backed Fabric.  3M Foamfast 73 (not sure if it is 73 or 78) is made for this.  It sprays on like silly string.  Spary it onto both surfaces and you have about ten minutes to move it around before the glue takes a permanent set.  I bought the fabric at a fabric store for around $7.50 per yard.  <p>A quick fix for sagging headliners is to use upholstry twist pins.  They look like buttons with a corkscrew shaped pin.  Just screw it into the headliner.  I did this to a van I was selling and it looked like custom interior.  WalMart carries them in a pack of ten for around $1.80 in the sewing area.<p>Good luck,<p>Carroll
Z||000005||Cooter||07-30-99||10:52 PM||||I had my headliner fixed at a local shop for $30 and they covered the sunvisers to match. For that much money, it would be tough for me to even want to do it myself.
Z||000006||lowCG||07-31-99||12:57 AM||||Well my 3M #s were off by a few,I used #77,as I now can see the label,mists from the can.<br>Fun for some of us who enjoy a less than grimy project to offset the rest of those knuckle busters jobs.
Z||000007||Mark||07-31-99||09:18 PM||||Just finished the headliner replacement, and it looks great.  I have a good feeling about it this time since I didn't stretch the fabric at all. I just kinda "rolled it" into place, starting from the center and working one side at a time (I had a helper to hold one side of the material).  After it was all pressed into place and trimmed, I placed soft pillows in the two dished areas with some books on them for weight (overnight) just to make absolutely sure I have good adhesion.  Total cost: fabric $12, glue (3M Super Trim Adhesive 08090) $15.  Local shops in the Pittsburgh were asking $50 to $90 if I R&R the headliner (about $120 if they di it all).  
Z||000008||batboy||07-31-99||09:31 PM||||Good job Mark...wanna do one for me now?
